1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,412
One part tells you almost nothing. Many parts obey a law. Start with

2
00:00:05,512 --> 00:00:10,844
the simplest part there is: one die, six faces, all equally likely.

3
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:14,594
Ten rolls tell you nothing: the worst face is out

4
00:00:14,694 --> 00:00:19,353
by two hundred percent. Keep rolling, and watch that distance.

5
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:24,452
Ten thousand rolls, and the worst face is within a few percent of

6
00:00:24,552 --> 00:00:29,790
one sixth. Nobody arranged that. It is what randomness does in bulk.

7
00:00:29,883 --> 00:00:34,012
Predictable in bulk. But this is still one die at a time,

8
00:00:34,112 --> 00:00:37,871
and one die is flat. Nothing here looks like a bell.

9
00:00:39,266 --> 00:00:44,926
One die is the baseline: every face equally likely, no shape at all.

10
00:00:45,016 --> 00:00:49,500
Average two of them and the flat top is already gone.

11
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,484
Average five, and there is a shape where there was none.

12
00:00:53,583 --> 00:00:56,669
Thirty, and it is a bell. Nothing about a

13
00:00:56,769 --> 00:01:00,011
die is bell shaped. The averaging did this.

14
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:04,567
Two numbers, measured separately, agreeing to three decimals:

15
00:01:04,667 --> 00:01:07,637
the spread of the averages, and one die's

16
00:01:07,737 --> 00:01:10,632
spread divided by the root of the count.

17
00:01:10,716 --> 00:01:16,568
That agreement is the law. Sigma of x bar equals sigma over root n.

18
00:01:18,066 --> 00:01:21,801
Put subgroup size along the bottom and the spread of

19
00:01:21,901 --> 00:01:25,046
the mean up the side, with sigma set to one.

20
00:01:25,133 --> 00:01:29,187
Now walk the subgroup size up from one to twenty five and

21
00:01:29,287 --> 00:01:33,049
read the spread of the mean off the curve as it goes.

22
00:01:33,133 --> 00:01:37,188
Twenty five parts, and the spread of the mean is a fifth

23
00:01:37,288 --> 00:01:41,121
of one part's spread. The root is doing all the work.

24
00:01:41,216 --> 00:01:44,404
But look at the shape of what you are buying.

25
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:48,827
The second part halves your uncertainty. The twenty fifth part

26
00:01:48,927 --> 00:01:52,755
buys four thousandths of a sigma. Averaging is cheap at

27
00:01:52,855 --> 00:01:56,040
the start and almost free of value at the end.

28
00:01:56,133 --> 00:01:58,917
Which is why subgroups of four and five

29
00:01:59,017 --> 00:02:02,393
are everywhere, and subgroups of fifty are not.

30
00:02:02,483 --> 00:02:06,923
And it is why a control chart plots subgroup means. A shift

31
00:02:07,023 --> 00:02:11,695
that hides inside single parts moves a mean far enough to see.

